---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/sequenzy-vs-yotpo-email-sms';
const title = 'Sequenzy vs retired Yotpo Email & SMS: Shopify continuity (2026)';
const desc = 'Evaluate Sequenzy for Shopify campaign continuity after Yotpo Email & SMS retirement: permission, order truth, queue handoff, and total economics.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can I choose Yotpo Email & SMS for a new campaign?",
    "answer": "No. Yotpo announced Email and SMS retirement for December 31, 2025. Its current SMS notice says the dashboard and background services are inactive. Treat old workflows as historical specifications."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Sequenzy only an email companion?",
    "answer": "No. Sequenzy advertises Shopify commerce workflows, email, web and mobile push, and in-app messaging. SMS and MMS are a paid US/Canada toll-free beta add-on; confirm account eligibility and sender approval."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I export my old Yotpo audience now?",
    "answer": "Do not assume access. Inventory files already lawfully retained and ask support what recovery is available. The current decommission notice does not guarantee exports, dashboard access, sender portability, or historical data recovery."
  },
  {
    "question": "Do Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty retire too?",
    "answer": "They are separate products. Review their current contracts and integration options independently; keeping them does not restore the retired messaging service or establish a working connection to a replacement sender."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I automatically choose a migration partner?",
    "answer": "Yotpo named Attentive and Omnisend in its 2025 migration guidance. That historical recommendation does not establish present terms, availability, or fit. Compare current requirements and quotes alongside other eligible options."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should purchaser exits work?",
    "answer": "Use Shopify order truth and a tested identity map. Check again before sending, cancel queued promotions after purchase, and deduplicate retries. Verify observed behavior in the replacement account instead of assuming parity with old flows."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a migrated phone number prove SMS permission?",
    "answer": "No. Retain channel-specific permission evidence, disclosures, timestamps, source, and suppression history. Quarantine uncertain records. Push permission and email permission do not establish SMS permission."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should costs be compared?",
    "answer": "Match audience eligibility, send mix, peak volume, service scope, and evaluation period. Include email volume, SMS/MMS usage, sender costs, retained apps, migration labor, support, and overlapping contracts. Historical invoices are a baseline, not a current Yotpo quote."
  },
  {
    "question": "When should the new sender go live?",
    "answer": "After permission, identity, order sync, purchase exits, queue retirement, retry deduplication, sender approval, and handoff tests pass. Assign an owner to pause sending and preserve new suppressions if rollback is needed."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><span>Sequenzy vs Yotpo Email &amp; SMS</span></nav>
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Historical comparison and continuity planning</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Sequenzy vs retired Yotpo Email &amp; SMS</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">For former Yotpo messaging users, the decision is how to rebuild permitted campaigns in a current service. Yotpo Email &amp; SMS is retired; it is no longer an active alternative for a new campaign.</p>
        <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-8">Sequenzy is a candidate when its Shopify workflows, channel reach, operating controls, and total costs match your requirements. A safe transition depends on recoverable evidence and tested account behavior.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox picks={[
          { label: 'Current candidate', tool: 'Sequenzy', reason: 'Evaluate Shopify workflows and eligible channels with a controlled pilot' },
          { label: 'Historical system', tool: 'Yotpo Email & SMS', reason: 'Use retained specifications and evidence; do not plan new sends on the retired product' },
          { label: 'Separate decision', tool: 'Reviews and Loyalty', reason: 'Assess retained Yotpo products and integrations independently' },
        ]} />
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>What changed, and what remains uncertain</h2>
        <p>Yotpo’s <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">September 2025 migration announcement</a> scheduled full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025 and named Attentive and Omnisend as partners. The <a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">current decommission notice</a> says the SMS dashboard and background services are inactive. Those earlier migration offers do not guarantee present support, export access, pricing, or number portability. Ask what is still recoverable before promising a timetable.</p>
        <p>Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty are separate products. Keeping either may be sensible when its own contract and business value hold up. It does not preserve the retired sender. Verify event availability, permitted use, identity mapping, and current integration support before using review or loyalty data in any replacement.</p>
        <h2>Current Sequenzy scope</h2>
        <p>The <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/sequenzy">Shopify listing</a> describes customer, order, product, checkout and inventory sync, campaign segmentation, and commerce flows. Its <a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/pricing">pricing page</a> uses email volume with unlimited contacts and advertises web, iOS and Android push plus in-app messaging. SMS/MMS is a paid US/Canada toll-free beta add-on. Check destination coverage, sender approval, usage billing and account access before including texts in a launch plan.</p>
        <p>These are advertised capabilities checked November 25, 2026, not evidence of this store’s integration latency, delivery, migration completeness or campaign results. Push and in-app require their own implementation and reachable audience. If geography, sender requirements or critical controls fail the pilot, evaluate another currently available provider against the same tests.</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Sequenzy evaluation</th><th>Former Yotpo continuity</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Campaign ownership</td><td>Test current workflows and permissions</td><td>Reconstruct approved intent from retained specifications</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Channels</td><td>Email, push and in-app; eligible paid SMS/MMS beta</td><td>Retired Email/SMS cannot be the new sending plan</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Customer records</td><td>Validate Shopify identity and order sync</td><td>Recover lawful records where available; quarantine gaps</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reviews and rewards</td><td>Verify actual integration and event semantics</td><td>Evaluate separate Reviews/Loyalty contracts</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Economics</td><td>Quote matched volume and scope</td><td>Use historical invoices only as a baseline</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <h2>Fourteen proposed merchant fixtures</h2>
        <p>Every merchant below is fictional. These are proposed acceptance fixtures, not customer stories, measured outcomes or vendor rankings. Rebuild only the jobs the merchant needs, using the controls below in each fixture.</p>
        <h3>Bloom & Wick: Holiday gifting</h3>
        <p>Separate gift buyers from self-purchasers; test teaser and shipping-deadline branches against fulfillment cutoffs, with one owner approving each channel’s calendar.</p>
        <h3>Peak Trail Gear: Clearance eligibility</h3>
        <p>Use a documented bargain-sensitive segment and hold out comparable eligible customers; exclude full-price buyers from the discount branch and measure contribution rather than assuming an offer wins.</p>
        <h3>Sauce Lab: Batch scarcity</h3>
        <p>Trigger a limited hot-sauce alert only from verified sellable stock. Reject stale inventory events and stop the pending alert when the batch sells out.</p>
        <h3>Ember Ceramics: Kiln waitlist priority</h3>
        <p>Rebuild an ordered pottery waitlist from available permission records. Test fair access and fulfillment capacity; an old queue position must not silently become permission to text.</p>
        <h3>Ridge Run Coffee: Subscription collision</h3>
        <p>Suppress active subscribers from one-off acquisition offers using current subscription truth; test delayed status updates and purchases arriving between eligibility calculation and dispatch.</p>
        <h3>Cipher Audio: Full-price product launch</h3>
        <p>Plan education followed by optional launch urgency for an eligible audience. Test browse-to-customer identity joins and cancel the launch reminder when the shopper buys; assume no margin improvement.</p>
        <h3>Patchwork Denim: One-of-one matching</h3>
        <p>Match style preferences to available unique pieces. Recheck the exact variant before dispatch and retire queued links when that item sells.</p>
        <h3>Arena Energy: Offer separation</h3>
        <p>Keep an email bundle offer distinct from an optional flavor-drop text. Approve terms and cross-channel frequency centrally so a retry cannot create a second incentive.</p>
        <h3>Loom & Frame: Edition disclosure</h3>
        <p>Use truthful edition counts and a verified sell-through threshold. Suppress collectors who already purchased this edition and deduplicate repeated threshold events.</p>
        <h3>Driftwood Surf Co: Size-specific restock</h3>
        <p>Route boardshort restock requests by size and variant, preserving request context. Test partial inventory arrivals and do not widen a waitlist alert to the whole audience.</p>
        <h3>Noir Collective: VIP release windows</h3>
        <p>Validate VIP eligibility and access timing against the store clock and recipient sending windows. Test daylight-saving boundaries and cancel the general reminder after early purchase.</p>
        <h3>SoleDrop ATX: Sneaker size-run queue</h3>
        <p>Test ordered access by requested size, with expiry and stock reservation rules owned by commerce operations. Prevent replayed invitations after queue handoff.</p>
        <h3>Festival Threads Co: Tour-city routing</h3>
        <p>Use declared city preference and venue dates without inferring text permission from location. Reapprove rescheduled events and retire messages for cancelled shows.</p>
        <h3>Vinyl Vault Records: Collector access</h3>
        <p>Define collector eligibility from verified purchase history and pressing availability. Recalculate after refunds and identity merges; keep early-access rules distinct from discount entitlement.</p>
        <h2>Permission and identity before activation</h2>
        <p>Inventory lawfully retained exports, consent receipts, suppression lists, templates and workflow specifications. Do not assume a retired dashboard can supply missing history. Reconcile email, normalized phone, Shopify customer ID, guest checkout and merged profiles without treating shared contact details as proof of identity. Retain channel, source, timestamp, disclosure and withdrawal evidence; quarantine ambiguous records instead of reconstructing permission. Review applicable sending rules and recipient-local restrictions with qualified advice. Email, SMS and push permissions are distinct.</p>
        <p>Exercise opt-outs, STOP/HELP handling where applicable, complaints, quiet hours, frequency limits and uncertain timezones in the actual account. Keep unresolved identities and permissions out of the pilot. See <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent planning</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a> for operational context.</p>
        <h2>Order truth, queue retirement and handoff</h2>
        <p>Use Shopify order IDs and current order status as commerce truth. Test purchase exits at enrollment, during delays and immediately before dispatch. Include refunds, cancellations, duplicate webhooks and out-of-order events; choose the correct exit rule for each job rather than restarting promotions on every refund. Record sync latency and fail closed when critical data is stale.</p>
        <p>Give each customer, campaign and event a stable deduplication key. Replay events and simulated timeouts to prove a retry does not send twice. Inventory all active replacement senders and any recoverable historical schedules; mark obsolete jobs retired in the transition ledger. Since Yotpo’s services are inactive, do not rely on being able to inspect or cancel queues there. Resolve unknown historical state with support where possible and avoid blindly replaying old backlog.</p>
        <p>Assign named owners for permission review, creative approval, sender approval, stock truth, support replies and incident response. Approve audience counts and exclusions, run a small permitted pilot, reconcile dispatch receipts, then hand off runbooks and alert ownership. Exactly one active workflow owns each job at cutover. Rollback means pause the replacement, preserve new opt-outs and deduplication history, and resume only a verified safe workflow after correction; it does not mean restoring the retired Yotpo sender.</p>
        <h2>Matched total economics</h2>
        <p>Compare the same eligible audience, evaluation period, channel mix, frequency, destination mix and peak-month volume. Obtain current quotes for email volume, SMS segments and MMS credits, sender provisioning, carrier charges where applicable, retained Reviews/Loyalty contracts, implementation, migration labor, support and overlapping contracts. Sequenzy is not a universally flat-priced plan; historical Yotpo invoices are not purchasable current messaging plans. Include required push and in-app implementation rather than treating advertised channels as an already reachable audience.</p>
        <p>Define contribution as net sales after discounts and returns, less goods, fulfillment, payment, messaging, platform and incremental labor costs. Do not subtract discounts or returns again if already included in net sales. Compare contribution per eligible customer with a comparable holdout over the same period; include fixed costs consistently and separate one-time transition expense from recurring operations. Attributed revenue alone is not incremental lift. See <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a>.</p>
        <h2>Conditional decision</h2>
        <p>Shortlist Sequenzy when its verified Shopify data, supported channels and account controls meet the merchant’s jobs at acceptable total cost. Keep separate Yotpo Reviews or Loyalty only when independently justified and tested. If essential records cannot be recovered, narrow the audience to demonstrably permitted contacts; if required reach or controls are unavailable, evaluate another current service. A safe continuity plan can pause uncertain jobs while preserving lawful suppression history.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16"><FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Sequenzy and retired Yotpo Email &amp; SMS FAQ" /></section>
</BaseLayout>
